From: RNSBox <rnsbox@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] file: call tzset() before strptime()

musl 1.2.6's strptime() implements %Z as

    strlen(tzname[0])

without calling tzset() first. tzname is only populated by tzset(), so a
process that never touched localtime() has tzname[0] == NULL and any
strptime() with %Z dereferences NULL.

uh_file_date2unix() parses If-Modified-Since / If-Unmodified-Since with
"%a, %d %b %Y %H:%M:%S %Z". A browser sends those headers for cached
CSS/JS on the second visit, and the NULL read killed uhttpd: the portal
went dead ("Unable to connect") with a SIGSEGV at strlen(NULL) inside
strptime(), even though the box itself kept running.

tzset() sets tzname to UTC when TZ and /etc/localtime are absent, and the
following strptime() then just skips the trailing "GMT" token.

---
 file.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/file.c b/file.c
--- a/file.c
+++ b/file.c
@@ -309,6 +309,14 @@ static time_t uh_file_date2unix(const char *date)
 {
 	struct tm t;
 
+	/*
+	 * musl's strptime("%Z") reads tzname[0] without calling tzset();
+	 * tzname stays NULL until tzset() runs, so a request carrying
+	 * If-Modified-Since (date + "GMT") crashed uhttpd on a NULL
+	 * dereference. Populate tzname first (UTC when no zone is set).
+	 */
+	tzset();
+
 	memset(&t, 0, sizeof(t));
 
 	if (strptime(date, "%a, %d %b %Y %H:%M:%S %Z", &t) != NULL)
